# `reaches(places[name], from)`: the table holds numbers and one list, and it is read under a key that nobody knows, so of the parameter Link holds a number or a list. The function subtracts from it and compares it: no name and no key is read off it, it is not called and not stepped through. Nothing bears out the list, so the turns are not built for a list behind a question in front of them, which a number would fail at every call: the value is asked whether it is a number where it is used.
# `fills(kept[side], ..)` is handed the same, and calls `push` off it, which lists alone have: there the uses bear out the list, and the question stays in front of the turns.
# f1 = reaches, f3 = fills
